And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. And he also used to say to people that they must be baptized and repent for their sins. And then God will forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him, and all the people from Jerusalem went out as well. When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach. "After me, one is coming who is mightier than I. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie His sandals."
Part of it is in water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit. Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. He was baptized in the Jordan River by John.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the heavens were opened. He saw the Holy Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven spoke to him and said, "You are my beloved son in whom I am well pleased."
At that moment, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. He was tempted by the devil. The wild beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After they put John in prison, Jesus went to Galilee. He preached the Gospel of the Lord of the Worlds.
He said, "The time has come. The kingdom of my Lord is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. As he walked, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
In a jar they shook their nets and followed it.
After a while Jesus walked on. As he walked he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in the boat preparing their nets.
Instead of resting, they went after him. Jesus expels an evil spirit.
Jesus and the people with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were amazed by his teachings. He taught them like someone with authority. He does not speak like someone teaching the law.
At this moment a man in their synagogue cried out, for he was possessed by an unclean spirit, and said,
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!"
"Be quiet," Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him."
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Afterwards, it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was amazed and began to ask one another, "What is this? A new teaching! And such mighty works accompany it! Even evil spirits are subject to it and obey it."
News about Jesus spread quickly throughout all of Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
And Jesus, with whom he had come out of the synagogue, left in a moment with Jacob and John, to the house of Simon and Andrew.
The old woman, Siman's wife, was lying in bed. She had a fever. They told her about Jesus.
He walked over to her, took her hand and helped her up. The heat left her. And then she started to prance around for them.
That night, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered in front of the door.
And Jesus healed many of them. They had various types of illnesses. And he also drove out many demons. But he did not allow them to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
The morning seemed very early and the world was still dark. Our Lord Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. He prayed there.
Simon and his companions went out to look for Jesus Christ.
When they found him, they called out, "The club is turning over on you."
Jesus said, "Let's go to another place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I need to preach there too. This is why I came."
So, he walked and traveled all over Galilee. And he was preaching in their synagogues. And he was also casting out demons. Jesus heals a man who has a skin disease.
A man with leprosy came to Jesus, kneeling before him, and said, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Yes, he was very worried. He reached out and touched the man. "I agree to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
In the place, the disease spread from it. It's done.
Fellis was not an enemy in an hour. And he gave the men a strong warning.
"Don't you dare speak of this to anyone," he said. "Go and present yourself to the Hajj. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will serve as testimony for the Hajj and the people to whom you are 'clean.'"
And so, the man went out and started talking very quickly. And he spread the news to everyone. And so, Jesus could no longer enter the city openly. He stayed outside in empty places. But people kept bringing him things from everywhere.
Jesus heals a servant who cannot walk.
After some days, Jesus entered again into Capernaum. The people heard that he was come into the house.
The crowd that gathered was so large that there was no more room. Even the area in front of the door was completely full. And Jesus announced their arrival to the crowd with a word.
Four people who came carried someone who wasn't moving.
They couldn't get close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug an opening through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on the mat down in front of Jesus.
Jesus saw the man and knew he believed. So he said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And he was going a little bit with the sharia scholars, and they were thinking.
Why is that man speaking like that? He's saying something very bad! Only God can forgive sins!
At that moment, Jesus knew what they were thinking about. Therefore, he said to them, "Why are you thinking about these things?"
Mother, isn't she telling this servant, "Your sins are forgiven," and not telling him, "Get up, take your bed and walk"?
I want to let you know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus spoke to the servant who could not walk.
He said, "We tell you," then he said, "Get up. Take your mattress and go to your house."
The man stood up, shook his mat, and walked away, with everyone following him with their eyes. All the people were amazed and praised God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus chooses Matthew.
Once again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd came and followed him. And he went to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting in the tax collector's booth. "Follow me," Jesus told him. Levi got up and followed him.
After this, Jesus was dining at the house of Levi. Many of the tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowds that followed Jesus.
So, the Pharisees, who were experts in religious law, were going along and saw Jesus eating with sinners and tax collectors. They asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard what had happened, and he said to them, "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners." When Jesus was asked about fasting,
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees were fasting; and they came and said to Him, "The disciples of John fast often and make a petition. Likewise, the disciples of the Pharisees fast carefully and make a petition. But Your disciples fast not. Why is this?"
Jesus said, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be neglected by them. On that day, they will go without food.
People don't sew a new patch on old clothes. If they did, the new patch would tear from the old. This would leave even more pieces.
People do not pour new wine into old wine skins. If they do, the wine will cause the skins to burst. Both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No, everyone must pour new wine into new wine skins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath day.
One Saturday afternoon, Jesus was walking with his disciples through fields of grain. The disciples began to pluck heads of wheat.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, this one is against the law to do this on the Sabbath. Why do your disciples do it?"
Answer, "Do you not know what David did when he and his men were hungry? They needed food."
This was in the time of Abiathar, the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate of the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his followers.
And Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
On that account, the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath."
Saturday is visible.
Jesus entered the synagogue, and there was a man with a withered hand.
Some Pharisees went looking for a mistake from Jesus. They observed him carefully. They wanted to know if he was going to heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man with the short and crooked arm. He told him: Stand in front of all the people.
And after Jesus, the Messiah, asked them and said to them, "What does the law say? Should we do good or evil on the Sabbath day, should we save life or kill?" But no one answered him.
And Jesus looked at them with anger. He was angry, their hearts were hard. And He said to the man, stretch out your hand. He stretched it out and his hand was healed.
After they went out, the Pharisees and Sadducees came and planned together to trap Jesus in what he said. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd of people from Galilee followed him.
The people heard all the things Jesus was doing. Large numbers of people came to him. They came from Judea, from Jerusalem, and from Idumea. They came from the region east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area of Tyre and Sidon.
Because many people, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small meal for him, this is so that he would not make the people crowd around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, sick people would pay to touch him.
When people who had evil spirits saw him, they fell down before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appointed the thirteen apostles.
Jesus went up on a mountain. He called certain people to come to him, and they came.
